<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ballenstedt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ballenstedt is a small town on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its castle stands on the site of an early medieval monastery associated with the first Ascanian counts, the ancestors of the house of Anhalt. In the 18th and 19th centuries the castle was the main residence of the princes and dukes of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, who also built a court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ballenstedt.jpg" alt="Ballenstedt" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolkenkratzer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ballenstedt_Detailansicht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ballenstedt is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> district of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. It lies at the northern rim of the Harz mountains, about 10 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, and is a stop on the Romanesque Road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Esico of Ballenstedt, who lived from about 1000 to 1059/60, is mentioned in a chronicle entry for 1030 and in a deed of Emperor Conrad II of 1036. He is considered the progenitor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>. In 1046, in the presence of Emperor Henry III, he had a collegiate church built in Ballenstedt. In 1123 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-rich">Otto the Rich</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>, who became the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, founded a Benedictine monastery on the site. Albert's grandson Henry I became the first Prince of Anhalt in 1218.</p><p>Prince Wolfgang of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521 and became one of the first Protestant rulers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The abbey was stormed and plundered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, and Wolfgang secularised it in 1525. He chose Ballenstedt as a residence and gave it town privileges in 1543. Imperial troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> plundered it in 1626, because the Anhalt princes had supported King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><p>After the war the princes of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> rebuilt the town and the former monastery as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> summer residence. In 1765 Prince Frederick Albert moved his residence from Bernburg to Ballenstedt Castle, which brought prosperity, including a castle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> built in 1788, the oldest theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, where Albert Lortzing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> are recalled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ballenstedt Castle is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> three-winged building and holds the grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>. Its park was designed by Peter Joseph Lenné. The town also keeps a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed Old Town Hall of 1683, the Late Gothic St Nicholas' Church, rebuilt in 1501, and parts of its town wall. Its coat of arms derives from the insignia of the counts of Ballenstedt, which is also the origin of the coat of arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ballenstedt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state was small and agrarian, with silver and copper mining in its Harz exclave around <b>Ballenstedt</b> and Harzgerode.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg</a></li></ul></section>
